Q: background-image: linear-gradient not working in Safari A: <p>This <strong>is</strong> related to iOS partially supporting <code>linear-gradient</code> as <code>background-image</code> prior to 15.3 version.</p>
<p>You can add color on top of an image using pseudo element:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.block::before {
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
  content: '';
  display: block;
  height: 100%;
  position: absolute;
  width: 100%;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And if you want to use this fallback only when it's needed, you can use <code>@support</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.block {
  background-image: linear-gradient(
    180deg,
    rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 0%,rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 100%),
    url(https://mypieceofcakemove.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/hero-bg-best-storage-nyc.jpg
  );
}

@supports not (background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 100%)) {
  .block::before {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
    content: '';
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    position: absolute;
    width: 100%;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here you check if certain rule is supported by a browser. If not, it uses provided rules.</p>
